text:
Demand for biofuels in the European Union continues to grow rapidly. According to estimates from Argus, consumption will reach 36.7 billion liters in 2026, compared with 30.6 billion liters a year earlier. By 2030, consumption could rise to 49 billion liters, 34% above the 2026 level. The forecast was presented by Kristin Yavorska, Senior Oilseed Market Analyst at international price reporting agency Argus, during the “Fat & Oil Industry 2026 European Edition” conference.

According to the speaker, the key drivers behind the expected growth include:

- RED III regulation. Under the EU’s new targets, renewable energy is expected to account for up to 29% of transport energy consumption by 2030 (or deliver a 14.5% reduction in greenhouse gas emissions). This is forcing fuel suppliers to procure larger volumes of renewable fuels;

- ILUC rules and changing feedstock preferences. The gradual phase-out of palm oil and restrictions on soybeans are shifting demand toward European feedstocks, primarily rapeseed oil and sunflower oil;

- The ReFuelEU Aviation mandate. Rising demand for sustainable aviation fuel (SAF) after 2030 is creating a new competitor for feedstocks, as SAF and hydrotreated vegetable oil (HVO) compete with biodiesel for the same resources amid tightening supplies of used cooking oils and animal fats.

“Taken together, these factors make the biofuel sector the main source of growth in demand for vegetable oils in Europe. At the same time, rapeseed is strengthening its position as the key feedstock for biofuel production,” Yavorska concluded.
